\ 1-x\\end{bmatrix},\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x+y=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+1 \\\\ 1+0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\notin L\n$$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (1)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 1-x\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad x\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x+y=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\quad {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+1 \\\\ 1+0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\notin L\n$$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (1)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\quad {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0}\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186564493 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $3x+y=4$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" "Let $L$ be the line with equation $3x+y=4$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186564493 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ -3x+4\\end{bmatrix},\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=-3x+4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (0) & =-3(0)+4 \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example,$$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+1 \\\\ 4+1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\notin L\n$$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (5) & =-3(1)+4 \\\\ 5 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ -3x+4\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad x\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=-3x+4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (0) & =-3(0)+4 \\\\ 0 & =4\\quad {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example,$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+1 \\\\ 4+1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\notin L\n$$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (5) & =-3(1)+4 \\\\ 5 & =1\\quad {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0}\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186564519 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $2x-y=3$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" "Let $L$ be the line with equation $2x-y=3$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186564519 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 2x-3\\end{bmatrix},\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $2x-y=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(0)-(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+1 \\\\ -3-1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\notin L\n$$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-(-4) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 2x-3\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad x\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $2x-y=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(0)-(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\quad {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+1 \\\\ -3-1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\notin L\n$$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-(-4) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\quad {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0}\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186649229 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $x=3y+2$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" "Let $L$ be the line with equation $x=3y+2$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186649229 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3y+2 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x=3y+2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3y+2 \\\\ (0) & =3(0)+2 \\\\ 0 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+5 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3y+2 \\\\ (7) & =3(1)+2 \\\\ 7 & =5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3y+2 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad y\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x=3y+2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3y+2 \\\\ (0) & =3(0)+2 \\\\ 0 & =2\\quad {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+5 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3y+2 \\\\ (7) & =3(1)+2 \\\\ 7 & =5\\quad {\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0}\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186120146 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(-2,2,-2)$ and $(3,-4,2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let $L$ be the line in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(-2,2,-2)$ and $(3,-4,2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186120146 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{5}=\\frac{y-2}{-6}=\\frac{z+2}{4}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)+2}{5}\\ne \\frac{(0)-2}{-6}\\ne \\frac{(0)+2}{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(1,-2,0)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n\\frac{(1)+2}{5}\\ne \\frac{(-2)-2}{-6}\\ne \\frac{(0)+2}{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” only." "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{5}=\\frac{y-2}{-6}=\\frac{z+2}{4}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)+2}{5}\\ne \\frac{(0)-2}{-6}\\ne \\frac{(0)+2}{4}.\\quad {\\color{red}\\times }\n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(1,-2,0)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n\\frac{(1)+2}{5}\\ne \\frac{(-2)-2}{-6}\\ne \\frac{(0)+2}{4}.\\quad {\\color{red}\\times }\n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $\\mathbf{0}\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186120151 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,-1,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let $L$ be the line in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,-1,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186120151 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx=-y=z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ -a+(-b) \\\\ a+b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ -(a+b) \\\\ a+b\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-y=z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ k(-a) \\\\ ka\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ -(ka) \\\\ ka\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-y=z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$L$ contains the zero-vector”; “$L$ is closed under vector addition”; “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” only." "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx=-y=z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ -a+(-b) \\\\ a+b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ -(a+b) \\\\ a+b\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-y=z.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot \\mathbf{a} & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ k(-a) \\\\ ka\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ -(ka) \\\\ ka\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-y=z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot \\mathbf{a}\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$L$ contains the zero-vector”; “$L$ is closed under vector addition”; “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” only."] [:db/cas 17592186275363 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,2,3)$ and $(3,2,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let $L$ be the line in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,2,3)$ and $(3,2,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186275363 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is false.The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{z-3}{-2},\\,y=2\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since the second coordinate of any point on this line satisfies\n\n$$\ny=2\\ne 0.\\quad{\\color{red}\\times} \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHowever, point $(4,4,4)$ does not belong to $L$ since it does not satisfy the equation of the line. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\ny=4\\ne 2.\\quad{\\color{red}\\times} \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” only." "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is false.The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{z-3}{-2},\\,y=2\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since the second coordinate of any point on this line satisfies\n\n$$\ny=2\\ne 0.\\quad{\\color{red}\\times} \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHowever, point $(4,4,4)$ does not belong to $L$ since it does not satisfy the equation of the line. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\ny=4\\ne 2.\\quad{\\color{red}\\times} \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $\\mathbf{0}\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186275390 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(2,1,-1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let $L$ be the line in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(2,1,-1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186275390 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ t \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2a+2b \\\\ a+b \\\\ (-a)+(-b)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(a+b) \\\\ a+b \\\\ -(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(2a) \\\\ ka \\\\ k(-a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(ka) \\\\ ka \\\\ -(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$L$ contains the zero-vector”; “$L$ is closed under vector addition”; “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” only." "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ t \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2a+2b \\\\ a+b \\\\ (-a)+(-b)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(a+b) \\\\ a+b \\\\ -(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot \\mathbf{a} & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(2a) \\\\ ka \\\\ k(-a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(ka) \\\\ ka \\\\ -(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot \\mathbf{a}\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$L$ contains the zero-vector”; “$L$ is closed under vector addition”; “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” only."] [:db/cas 17592186275391 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,-2,3).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let $L$ be the line in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,-2,3).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186275391 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the li