 t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}(-a)+(-b) \\\\ 3a+3b \\\\ a+b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b) \\\\ a+b\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(-a) \\\\ k(3a) \\\\ ka\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-(ka) \\\\ 3(ka) \\\\ ka\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-t \\\\ 3t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}(-a)+(-b) \\\\ 3a+3b \\\\ a+b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b) \\\\ a+b\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot \\mathbf{a} & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(-a) \\\\ k(3a) \\\\ ka\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-(ka) \\\\ 3(ka) \\\\ ka\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot \\mathbf{a}\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""] [:db/cas 17592186475137 :answer-field/key "$L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" "$L$ is a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186489255 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,-1,2)$ and $(-1,1,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" "Let $L$ be the line in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,-1,2)$ and $(-1,1,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186489255 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-2}{-1}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)-1}{-2}=\\frac{(0)+1}{2}\\ne \\frac{(0)-2}{-1}\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(0,0,3)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)-1}{-2}=\\frac{(0)+1}{2}\\ne -(3)+2.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-2}{-1}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)-1}{-2}=\\frac{(0)+1}{2}\\ne \\frac{(0)-2}{-1}\\quad {\\color{red}\\times }\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(0,0,3)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)-1}{-2}=\\frac{(0)+1}{2}\\ne -(3)+2.\\quad {\\color{red}\\times }\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0}\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186492036 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(2,1,2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" "Let $L$ be the line in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(2,1,2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186492036 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ t \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=y=\\frac{z}{2}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ 2b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ 2b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2a+2b \\\\ a+b \\\\ 2a+2b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(a+b) \\\\ a+b \\\\ 2(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(2a) \\\\ ka \\\\ k(2a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(ka) \\\\ ka \\\\ 2(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ t \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=y=\\frac{z}{2}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ 2b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ 2b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2a+2b \\\\ a+b \\\\ 2a+2b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(a+b) \\\\ a+b \\\\ 2(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot \\mathbf{a} & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ 2a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(2a) \\\\ ka \\\\ k(2a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(ka) \\\\ ka \\\\ 2(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot \\mathbf{a}\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186120152 :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-y-z=1$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let $\\Pi$ be the plane with equation $x-y-z=1$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186120152 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ x-y-1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$Π$ contains the zero-vector” is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (0) & =(0)-(0)-(1) \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+1 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ -1+0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (-1) & =(1)-(0)-1 \\\\ -1 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” and “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” only." "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ x-y-1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}x,y\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$\\Pi$ contains the zero-vector” is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (0) & =(0)-(0)-(1) \\\\ 0 & =-1\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$\\Pi$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\in \\Pi$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in \\Pi.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\en